(configuration-contribution-guide)=
## Configuration

Plugin settings allow your plugin to expose user-configurable options in
napari's **Preferences** dialog. They are declared in the
manifest under `contributions.configuration` and are rendered automatically —
no Python code is needed to build the settings UI.

Configuration contribution values are exposed at runtime via
`napari.settings.get_plugin_settings("example-plugin")` and persisted to
`<config dir>/example-plugin.yaml`. Settings are written to disk
*automatically* whenever a value changes.

### A minimal example

```yaml
{{ 'configuration'|example_contribution }}
```

Each entry in `contributions.configuration` is a **category** of settings. The
example above declares two categories, `Pre-processing Widget` and `Reader`, each with its
own properties.

### Categories, keys, and titles

Three pieces of metadata determine how a setting is shown and accessed:

- **`title` (of the category)** — the heading shown in the Preferences dialog
  for that category of settings (e.g. `Pre-processing Widget`, `Reader`).
  Avoid redundant words like "Plugin", "Configuration", or "Settings" in your
  category title. The title is **not** just for display: it is snake_cased and
  used as the attribute name for accessing that category programmatically
  (see the **key** bullet below).
- **`title` (of the property)** — the label shown next to the setting's input
  widget in the Preferences dialog (e.g. `Default colormap`). Each property must
  define a `title` for display in the Preferences dialog.
- **key** (the dictionary key of a property, e.g. `example-plugin.colormap`) —
  used to *namespace* the setting and to derive its internal identifier (the
  Python field name). Keys are **never shown in the UI**, but they form the
  attribute used to access the setting at runtime, nested under the category's
  snake_cased title:

  ```python
  from napari.settings import get_plugin_settings

  my_settings = get_plugin_settings("example-plugin")
  my_settings.pre_processing_widget.colormap  # <snake_cased category title>.<key>
  my_settings.reader.max_size_mb
  ```

The category title is converted to snake_case to form the attribute name
(`Pre-processing Widget` → `pre_processing_widget`, `Reader` → `reader`), and
each key becomes a snake_case field name on that model (with the plugin-name
prefix stripped).

### Events: Reacting to settings changes

Settings are napari `EventedModel`s, so changing a value emits a
[psygnal](https://github.com/pyapp-kit/psygnal) event that you can connect to.
Each category exposes an `events` emitter group with one emitter per setting:

```python
my_settings = get_plugin_settings("example-plugin")

def on_max_size_changed(event):
    print("max size is now", event.value)

my_settings.reader.events.max_size_mb.connect(on_max_size_changed)

my_settings.reader.max_size_mb = 1024  # event causes a print: "max size is now 1024"
```

The top-level settings object also re-emits every change as a single
`changed` event carrying a dotted `<category>.<property>` key, so one
connection covers all of a plugin's settings:

```python
my_settings.events.changed.connect(lambda e: print(e.key, e.value))
# after the assignment above -> "reader.max_size_mb 1024"
```

These events fire whether a value is changed in code or in the Preferences
dialog, and the change is auto-saved to the config file.

### Supported types and constraints

Each property follows a subset of
[JSON Schema (draft 2020-12)](https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/). The
`type` of a property is one of four JSON Schema types:

| `type` | Widget in Preferences | Meaning | Extra options |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `boolean` | checkbox | `true` / `false` | |
| `string` | line edit | any text | `enum` → dropdown; `minLength` / `maxLength`; `is_multiline` |
| `integer` | integer spin box | whole numbers | `minimum` / `maximum` / `exclusiveMinimum` / `exclusiveMaximum` / `multipleOf` |
| `number` | float spin box | real numbers (floats) | `minimum` / `maximum` / `exclusiveMinimum` / `exclusiveMaximum` / `multipleOf` |

`number` is JSON Schema's type for **floats** (any real number); `integer` is
for whole numbers. npe2 also accepts the Python type names `bool`, `int`,
`float`, and `str`, so `type: float` is equivalent to `type: number`.

`enum` is supported on **any** property type (it renders a dropdown), not just
strings. Both YAML styles are valid — a flow list (`enum: [gray, green,
viridis]`) or a block list (as in the example above); the block style is
clearer when there are several options.

A `default` is required for every property. Constraints are enforced by the
settings model, so invalid values cannot be saved.

### How settings render in napari

For the example plugin above, the Preferences dialog shows one page per
plugin, with each category rendered as its own group:

```text
Preferences
  ...              ── existing napari sections ──
  ----Plugin Preferences----
  Example Plugin   ◄── page per plugin (display_name)

  ╔═ Pre-processing Widget ══════════════╗   ◄── category title
  ║  Default colormap          [dropdown] ║   ◄── property title + widget
  ║  Gamma correction               [1.0] ║
  ╚══════════════════════════════════════╝
  ╔═ Reader ═════════════════════════════╗
  ║  Max in-memory image size (MB)  [512] ║
  ║  Auto-contrast on open            [x] ║
  ╚══════════════════════════════════════╝
```

The `title` of each category becomes a group-box header, and each property
renders as a labelled input (`title:` label + widget chosen by `type:`). The
property `description:` becomes the widget's help/tooltip text.

### Validation

npe2 validates that configuration **titles** and **property keys** are unique
within a plugin (after normalization to snake_case), and that every property
has a valid type, default, and constraints. Invalid manifests are rejected at
parse time, so mistakes surface early rather than as a broken Preferences page.

### Testing your settings

In a normal napari session your installed plugin is discovered and
`get_plugin_settings("example-plugin")` just works. Under **pytest** it
doesn't: npe2's pytest plugin blocks discovery so that test suites stay
hermetic, and napari caches settings for the lifetime of the process. So in
a test, register your plugin's manifest with the test
plugin manager and reset the cache before calling `get_plugin_settings`:

```python
def test_my_settings(tmp_path):
    from napari import settings as napari_settings
    from npe2 import PluginManager, PluginManifest

    pm = PluginManager.instance()
    if 'example-plugin' not in pm:
        pm.register(PluginManifest.from_distribution('example-plugin'))
    # Reset the in-memory cache so the registered plugin is included (saved
    # values are re-read from disk; this also allows path_dir=tmp_path).
    napari_settings._PLUGIN_PREFERENCES.clear()

    s = napari_settings.get_plugin_settings('example-plugin', path_dir=tmp_path)
    assert s.reader.max_size_mb == 512
    assert s.config_path.name == 'example-plugin.yaml'

    s.reader.max_size_mb = 1024  # auto-saves to the config file
    assert 'max_size_mb: 1024' in s.config_path.read_text()
```

### Supporting older napari

Declaring `contributions.configuration` works on **every** napari — npe2
parses the manifest regardless of version, so an older napari simply ignores
the block (the settings just don't appear in Preferences). Only the runtime
*read* API, `get_plugin_settings`, is available in napari>=0.9.0.

If you need to support older napari, feature-detect the API and fall back to
defaults — you don't need a full settings shim, only the values your code
actually reads. In this way, older napari will not enable customizing the settings.

```python
from types import SimpleNamespace

# Defaults mirroring your manifest (only the values your code reads).
_DEFAULTS = SimpleNamespace(
    reader=SimpleNamespace(max_size_mb=512, auto_contrast=True),
)

def my_settings():
    """Return napari's settings, or defaults on napari < 0.9.0."""
    try:
        from napari.settings import get_plugin_settings
        return get_plugin_settings("example-plugin")
    except ImportError:  # pragma: no cover - napari < 0.9.0
        return _DEFAULTS
```

If you only read one or two settings, inline the fallback instead:

```python
def max_size_mb() -> float:
    try:
        from napari.settings import get_plugin_settings
        return get_plugin_settings("example-plugin").reader.max_size_mb
    except (ImportError, KeyError):
        return 512.0
```
